# Folder shapes design

Which files and folders a project folder may hold, and what Fix changes in a project folder.
A project folder is the folder of a registered project of any kind (see ControllersDesign).

## Desks

```
D ::= ids; folders
folder ::= id{Es}
E ::= s -> entry
entry ::= id | file | other
p ::= id s1..sn
s ::= U0..Un
```
- In a D, the ids of the folders are disjoint.
- In a D, every id of the ids, and every id of an E of the folders, is the id of a folder of the folders.
- In a folder, the s of the Es are disjoint.

`U`, `file` and `other` are atoms.
The ids of a D are the roots: the paths start there.
```
at(D, id)= id                 with D= _ id _; _
at(D, p s)= entry             with at(D, p)= id, id{_ s -> entry _} in D
create(D, p s, file)= ids; folders id{Es s -> file} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
                          at(D, p s) undefined
```
A 'checkable folder property' is a predicate on Ds and paths defined using only `at` and `create`, never comparing two ids.
```
valid(D, p)         iff  at(D, p)= id
same(D, p1, p2)     iff  valid(D, p1), valid(D, p2),
                         exists s, file: at(D, p1 s) undefined, at(D, p2 s) undefined, at(create(D, p1 s, file), p2 s)= file
inside(D, p1, p2)   iff  valid(D, p1), valid(D, p2), exists p: p1 < p, same(D, p, p2)
p s0..sn < p
```

## Entries

An 'entry' is a file or a folder inside the project folder.
A 'zip' is a file whose name ends with `.zip`, when every name from the project folder to that file starts with a character other than `.`.
A 'zip' `x.zip` stands for a folder `x` holding the content of the 'zip': a file or a folder inside a 'zip' is an 'entry' too.
The 'path' of an 'entry' is the names from the project folder to the 'entry', joined with `/`, where each 'zip' `x.zip` on the way counts as `x`: the file `a.fear` of `lib/x.zip` has the 'path' `lib/x/a.fear`.
An 'entry' is 'invisible' when a name of the 'path' of the 'entry' starts with `.`.
Any other 'entry' is 'visible'.

## Shape rules

A project folder is 'well shaped' when every 'shape rule' of this section holds.
A code project or a data project whose project folder is not 'well shaped' is 'invalid' (see Project states in ControllersDesign); the 'problem' names one broken 'shape rule' and the 'path' breaking that 'shape rule'.
The code checking the 'shape rules' is `Coordinator/src/realSourceOracle`.

### Every entry

- The project folder is a folder.
- An 'entry' on the disk is a regular file or a folder: not a symbolic link, not a junction, not any other kind of object of the file system.

### Visible entries

A 'valid segment' holds only lowercase letters (`a` to `z`), digits and `_`, starts with a letter or `_`, and is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `com1` to `com9` or `lpt1` to `lpt9`.
A 'valid file name' is one of:
- a 'valid segment', a dot and an extension of 1 to 16 lowercase letters or digits;
- a 'valid segment', a dot and one of `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz`, `tar.zst`;

- The name of a 'visible' folder is a 'valid segment'.
- The name of a 'visible' file is a 'valid file name'.
- The 'path' of a 'visible' 'entry' is at most 200 characters long.
- A 'visible' folder holds at least one 'entry', 'visible' or 'invisible'; so does the project folder.
- The time of a 'visible' file is after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and at most 5 seconds after the current time. The time of a file on the disk is the last modified time; the time of a file inside a 'zip' is the time the 'zip' stores for that file.

### Invisible entries

- The name of an 'invisible' 'entry' does not end with a dot or a space.
- The name of an 'invisible' 'entry' holds no control character, no surrogate without its pair, and none of `<`, `>`, `:`, `"`, `/`, `\`, `|`, `?`, `*`.
- The part of the name of an 'invisible' 'entry' before the first dot, in lowercase, is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `com1` to `com9` or `lpt1` to `lpt9`.
- No two 'invisible' 'entries' of one folder have names that are equal after Unicode normalization (NFC) and lowercasing.
- An 'invisible' folder that is not inside another 'invisible' folder holds at least one 'entry'.
- The 'path' of an 'invisible' 'entry' is at most 250 characters long. The limit for the files of the user is 200 characters, and the rules at the end of every error on a 'shape rule' tell the user 200 characters for every 'entry'; the 50 characters more are for the files that Fearless generates inside the project folder, like the files of `.fearless_out`.
Nothing else is checked about an 'invisible' 'entry': an 'invisible' folder inside another 'invisible' folder may hold no 'entry', an 'invisible' file may have any time, and an 'invisible' file `x.zip` is a file like any other, on the disk and inside a 'zip'.

### Zips

- A 'zip' is a readable zip file.
- A 'zip' holds at least one 'entry'.
- The names a 'zip' stores are UTF-8.
- A name a 'zip' stores is not empty, does not start with `/`, holds no NUL character, and holds no segment that is empty, `.` or `..`.
- A 'zip' stores no name twice.
- A 'zip' stores no file under the name of another file of that 'zip'.
- A 'zip' `x.zip` has no sibling named `x`.
- A 'zip' of the disk holds at most 64 levels of 'zips' inside 'zips'.
The files and folders inside a 'zip' are 'entries': the rules of Visible entries and Invisible entries hold for them.

## Fix

Fix is an operation of the manager on one project (`Fix <name>` in ControllersDesign).
Fix changes the content of the project folder, and nothing else.
A 'desk file' is one of the following 'entries', 'visible' or 'invisible':
- a file named `.DS_Store`;
- a file whose name starts with `._`;
- a folder named `__MACOSX` or `.AppleDouble`, with every 'entry' inside that folder;
- a file named `Icon` followed by a carriage return;
- a file named `Thumbs.db`, `ehthumbs.db`, `ehthumbs_vista.db` or `desktop.ini`, in any mix of uppercase and lowercase;
- a file whose name ends with `:Zone.Identifier`;
- a file named `.directory`;
- a folder whose name starts with `.Trash-`, with every 'entry' inside that folder;
- a file whose name ends with `~`;
- a file whose name starts with `~$`;
- a file whose name starts with `.~lock.` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `#` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `.` and ends with `.swp`.
The desktops, the archive programs, the office programs and the text editors write the 'desk files' on their own, next to the files of the user.

The 'fixed segment' of a text is that text in lowercase, with every sequence of characters other than `a` to `z`, digits and `_` turned into one `_`, and with a leading `p` when the result is not a 'valid segment'.
The 'guessed extension' of a file is:
- `png` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A`;
- `jpg` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `FF D8 FF`;
- `gif` when the content of the file starts with the text `GIF87a` or `GIF89a`;
- `txt` when the content of the file is UTF-8 text holding no control character other than tab, line feed and carriage return; a file with no content is such a file;
- `bin` otherwise.
The 'fixed name' of a folder is the 'fixed segment' of the name of the folder.
The 'fixed name' of a file is the 'fixed segment' of the stem of the file, a dot and the extension of the file, where the stem is the name without the dot and the extension, and the extension is:
- `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz` or `tar.zst`, when the name in lowercase ends with a dot and that extension;
- otherwise the end of the name after the last dot, in lowercase, when that end is 1 to 16 letters (`a` to `z`, `A` to `Z`) or digits;
- otherwise the 'guessed extension' of the file; the stem is then the whole name.
Two names 'clash' when the two names are equal after lowercasing and after removing an ending `.zip`.

Fix visits every 'entry' once, in the order of the names; Fix visits the 'entries' of a folder or of a 'zip' between step 4 and step 5 on that folder or 'zip'. Fix does the following to each 'entry', in this order:
1. Fix deletes every 'desk file'.
2. Fix renames to the 'fixed name' every 'visible' folder whose name is not a 'valid segment' and every 'visible' file whose name is not a 'valid file name'. When the 'fixed name' 'clashes' with the name of another 'entry' of the same folder, Fix adds at the end of the 'fixed segment' the first of `2`, `3`, `4`... giving a name that 'clashes' with the name of no other 'entry' of that folder.
3. Fix cleans the text of every 'visible' file with the extension `fear`. The text of the file is the UTF-16 text when the file starts with a UTF-16 byte order mark (the bytes `FF FE` or `FE FF`), the UTF-8 text when the bytes of the file are UTF-8, and the windows-1252 text otherwise. In that text Fix turns U+2018 and U+2019 into `'`, U+201C and U+201D into `"`, and U+2013, U+2014 and U+2212 into `-`. Fix then writes the file again as the result of the run of `StrCleaners.softText` on the UTF-8 bytes of the text (see TextCleanersDesign). The Frontend refuses a source file holding a character other than the line feed and U+0020 to U+007E.
4. Fix sets to the current time the time of every file, 'visible' or 'invisible', whose time is not after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z or is more than 5 seconds after the current time.
5. Fix deletes every folder that holds no 'entry' and is not inside an 'invisible' folder, and every 'zip' holding no 'entry'; Fix repeats this step until no such folder and no such 'zip' is left. Fix never deletes the project folder, and never deletes a folder inside an 'invisible' folder for holding no 'entry': git needs the empty folders of `.git`.
Fix changes or deletes no other 'entry': a project folder breaking another 'shape rule' is not 'well shaped' after Fix either.
Fix does to the 'entries' inside a 'zip' what Fix does to the 'entries' on the disk, in the same order: Fix writes again every 'zip' that Fix changes, and the files that Fix does not change keep their names, their content, their times and their order in that 'zip'.
Before visiting the 'entries' of a 'zip', Fix changes the names that 'zip' stores: every `\` becomes `/`, then Fix removes a `/` at the start of a name, every `/` following another `/`, and every segment `.`; a folder whose name becomes empty is no longer stored.
Fix leaves as it is a 'zip' that is not a readable zip file, a 'zip' storing a name that still breaks a 'shape rule' of Zips after that change of the names, and a 'zip' deeper than 64 levels.

Fix empties the Output of the project, then writes in the Output one line for each change, with the 'path' of the 'entry' changed, like `Deleted lib/.DS_Store`, `Renamed img/My Logo.PNG to my_logo.png`, `Removed the byte order mark of _app/main.fear`, `Fixed the line endings of _app/main.fear`, `Cleaned 3 characters of _app/main.fear`, `Fixed the stored names of lib/old.zip`, `Reset the time of img/logo.png`, `Deleted the empty folder old` and `Deleted the empty zip lib/old.zip`.
The line of a renamed 'entry' holds the 'path' before the renaming; the lines of the later changes hold the 'path' after the renaming.
The number of cleaned characters is the number of characters of the text, after the byte order mark is removed and the line endings are fixed, other than the line feed and U+0020 to U+007E.
When Fix changes nothing, the Output says `The project "hello" needs no fix.`.
A change that the file system refuses ends Fix: the Output holds the lines of the changes already done, then the error of the file system with the path on the disk that the file system refuses.
Fix ends the same way instead of renaming an 'entry' to a name that the file system takes as the name of another 'entry': a file system can take as one name two names that do not 'clash'. The Output holds the lines of the changes already done, then `file already exists:` and the path on the disk of that name.
Fix ends the same way at a folder holding a symbolic link or a junction: the Output holds the lines of the changes already done, then `This path is a symbolic link/junction, and Fearless does not follow symbolic links/junctions:` and the path on the disk of the symbolic link or junction.

## Implementation notes

### Linux

- U: a byte other than `0x00` and `0x2F` (`/`).
- No s is `.` or `..`.
- ids: the mount points, field 5 of `/proc/self/mountinfo` with the `\ooo` escapes decoded; `/` is one of them.
- The text `/s1/../sn` is the path `id sk+1..sn`, with `/s1/../sk` the longest mount point it starts with, and `id` that mount point.
- The s of the folder `/s1/../sn`: the names `find /s1/../sn -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -printf '%f\0'` gives, separated by NUL.
- The entry of each s, by `stat -c %F`:
  - file: `regular file`, `regular empty file`;
  - folder: `directory`;
  - other: `symbolic link`, `fifo`, `socket`, `character special file`, `block special file`, any other.

### macOS

- U: a byte other than `0x00` and `0x2F` (`/`).
- No s is `.` or `..`.
- ids: the mount points `mount` lists, the path after `on`; `/` is one of them.
- The text `/s1/../sn` is the path `id sk+1..sn`, as on Linux.
- The s of the folder `/s1/../sn`: the names `find /s1/../sn -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -print0` gives, separated by NUL, each without `/s1/../sn/` in front.
- The entry of each s, by `stat -f %HT`:
  - file: `Regular File`;
  - folder: `Directory`;
  - other: `Symbolic Link`, `FIFO`, `Socket`, `Character Device`, `Block Device`, `Whiteout`, any other.

### Windows

- U: a 16 bit unit other than `0x0000`, `0x002F` (`/`) and `0x005C` (`\`).
- No s is `.` or `..`.
- ids: the paths `mountvol` lists for the volumes (`C:\`, and the folders where a volume is mounted), and every `\\server\share`.
- The text `r\s1\..\sn` is the path `id sk+1..sn`, with `r\s1\..\sk` the longest id it starts with.
- The s of the folder `r\s1\..\sn`: the names `Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath 'r\s1\..\sn' -Force -Name` gives.
- The entry of each s, by the attributes of `(Get-Item -LiteralPath <path> -Force).Attributes` and the tag and target `fsutil reparsepoint query <path>` gives:
  - file: no `Directory`, and no `ReparsePoint` or a cloud tag `0x9000x01A`;
  - folder: `Directory`, and no `ReparsePoint`, or a cloud tag `0x9000x01A`, or the tag `0xA0000003` with a target `\??\Volume{<guid>}\`;
  - other: the tag `0xA000000C`, the tag `0xA0000003` with any other target, any other tag, a device.
